Reside there constantly, having enjoyed
pleasures unattainable even by the gods;
meditate upon me continually through the discipline of devotion;
at the burning of the cosmic cycle you shall enter into me again.”

This valor of the dwarf has been recounted to you, O brahmins;
that Supreme Spirit always accomplishes the tasks of the gods.

Thus ends the sixteenth chapter in the former section of the compilation of six thousand verses in the glorious Kūrma Purāṇa.

From him came Svayaṃbhoja; from him Hṛdika, the tormentor of foes;
then his son was Kṛtavarman; Devara is remembered as his son;
that wise Śūra was his son, and Vasudeva was his son.

Homage be to Vāmadeva, Mahādeva, the creator,
to Śambhu, Sthāṇu, ever to Śiva, Parameṣṭhin;
homage to Soma, Rudra, the Great Devourer, the cause.

Jaimini said:
Lord, you ought truly to cut away this single doubt;
for there is nothing unknown to you, the supreme seer.

The Lord said:
“This higher knowledge cannot be spoken, this knowledge rejected by the ignorant;
I shall tell you according to truth that which has been declared by the supreme seers.

Freed from birth, death, and old age, he goes to the supreme abode of Śiva;
for that is the destination of those who desire liberation, never to die again,
attaining which he has achieved his purpose—so the learned consider.”

Siddhas and hosts of gods in the sky sang his praises;
charming divine apsarases danced;
Gandharvas, Vidyādharas, Kiṃnaras, and others
showered a rain of flowers mingled with water.

I go constantly for refuge to the lord of Guha;
I resort to Sthāṇu, Giriśa, the enemy of Tripura;
I resort to Śiva, Hara, who has the moon upon his crown,
to you, the wielder of the bow Pināka, I go for refuge.”

Then Śaṅkukarṇa, his self liberated, identical with that, all-pervading, spotless,
merged into the pure liṅga;
it was like a miracle.

He dwells in a dreadful hell for a myriad hundred kalpas;
therefrom turns [away no one, for] dreadful indeed is the anger of cattle;
and the ancestors of that embodied soul do not accept water [offered by him].

headed by Sanatkumāra, and other sage-brahmins,
serpent-deities, bird-deities, and Siddhas, continually dwell;
and Lord Hari resides there, honored by the Lord of Creatures.

Above the sphere of the sages, at just a hundred thousand, stands the Pole Star;
the Pole Star serves as the central pivot of the entire wheel of constellations.
There stands the blessed Dharma, Viṣṇu Nārāyaṇa.

Eight boundary mountains have been declared here by me, O twice-born;
Jaṭhara and the rest stand in the four directions of Meru, O great ṛṣis.

Thus ends the forty-fourth chapter in the first section of the compilation of six thousand verses in the auspicious Kūrma Purāṇa.

Sūta said:
In Ketumāla, the men are dark, all feeding on jackfruit;
and the women have the lustre of blue lotus petals, and they live for ten thousand years.

In Ramyaka, men and women delight themselves, radiant as silver;
established in purity, feeding on the fruit of the banyan tree,
they live for ten thousand and one thousand five hundred years.

In Hiraṇmaya, all have a golden hue and feed on breadfruit;
men and women live for eleven thousand and one thousand five hundred years,
just like dwellers in the world of the gods.

Thus has the extent of Jambūdvīpa been declared in brief;
it could not be described in detail by me even in hundreds of years.

Thus ends the forty-sixth chapter in the former section of the holy Kūrma Purāṇa, in the compilation of six thousand [verses].

Gomeda is said to be the first of them, and Candra the second;
Nārada, Dundubhi, Soma, and also Ṛṣabha;
Vaibhrāja is declared the seventh, exceedingly dear to Brahmā.

Maheśvara is beyond the unmanifest; the cosmic egg arises from the unmanifest;
from the cosmic egg Brahmā was born, and by him this world was created.

Thus ends the forty-eighth chapter in the first section of the compilation of six thousand verses in the glorious Kūrma Purāṇa.

In the twenty-seventh, the great sage Jātūkarṇa was Vyāsa;
and when this twenty-eighth Dvāpara age was reached, O twice-born,
the son of Parāśara, Kṛṣṇa Dvaipāyana, became Vyāsa.

He engaged Jaimini as the teacher of the Sāmaveda,
and likewise Sumantu, the best of sages, for the Atharvaveda;
and he appointed me to expound the histories and Purāṇas.

Devoted to the Veda, always [devoted to] the Lord, he knows that which is beyond the Veda to be supreme;
that Lord, Maheśvara, the embodiment of the Veda, is to be known through the Veda;
he alone is the Veda and the object of knowledge; resorting to him alone, one is liberated.

The Lord said:
From the unmanifest arose time, primordial matter, and the supreme spirit;
from them this entire [universe] was born; therefore the world is permeated by Brahman.

Higher than the breath of life is space; beyond space is the Lord, the fire;
I am he, all-pervading, tranquil, the self of knowledge, the Supreme Lord;
there is no being higher than me; having known me, one is liberated.

In my presence, this time creates the entire world;
the infinite-natured one impels it—this indeed is the teaching of the Veda.

Thus ends the third chapter in the latter section (the Īśvaragītā) in the compilation of six thousand verses of the glorious Kūrma Purāṇa.

Having washed hands and feet, while eating he should rinse twice;
seated in a clean place, after eating he should rinse twice again.

Thus ends the twelfth chapter in the latter section of the compilation of six thousand verses in the holy Kūrma Purāṇa.

Vyāsa said:
After eating, drinking, sleeping, bathing, walking on a public road,
touching one's lower lip, and changing clothes;

Upon speaking to a Caṇḍāla or a barbarian; upon speaking to a woman, a Śūdra, or one who is ritually defiled by food;
having touched a defiled person, or food of that sort;
or at the shedding of tears or blood, one should rinse one's mouth.

With the joining of the little finger and thumb he should touch the ears;
with the joining of all, or with the palm, he should touch the heart;
likewise he should touch the head, or with the thumb touch both.

If holding an unbaked earthen vessel he should become ritually defiled,
without setting down that item, having rinsed, he attains purity;
there is an option regarding clothes and the like: having touched them, he should rinse here.

He should not cause harm to another by means of water, wind, heat, and the like.
Having had his work done, he should not subsequently cheat the artisans;
in the evening or morning, he should not knock at house doors for the sake of begging.

Nor should he go without shoes, or destitute of water and other essentials,
nor at night, nor with an enemy, nor without a water pot;
nor should he ever pass between fire, a cow, Brahmins, and the like.

Duty and Vedic knowledge of Brahman do not arise from anywhere else;
therefore, the twice-born should place faith in duty and the Purāṇa.

Thus ends the twenty-fourth chapter in the latter section of the compilation of six thousand verses in the glorious Kūrma Purāṇa.

The householder should be known as two-fold: the practitioner and the non-practitioner.
Teaching, officiating at sacrifices, and receiving gifts are mentioned for the former;
he may carry out usury, agriculture, and commerce, [provided they are] not done by himself.

He should not eat anything at night; at night he should be devoted to meditation,
his senses conquered, his anger conquered, contemplating the knowledge of reality;
he should always be celibate and should not consort even with his wife.

He may endure the five fires, or drink smoke, or consume warm food, or drink soma;
he should drink milk during the bright fortnight, and [consume] cow dung during the dark fortnight,
or eat fallen leaves, or sustain himself always with severe penances.

He should simply await his time, as a servant awaits a command;
he should never study, nor speak, nor listen;
knowing thus, the supreme yogin is fit for becoming Brahman.

Or the wise man may wear a single garment, or wear only a loincloth;
he may be shaved-headed or wear a crest; he should carry the triple staff, possess nothing,
always wear ochre robes, and be intent on the yoga of meditation.

He should dwell at the edge of a village, at the root of a tree, or in a temple of the gods;
equal toward enemy and friend, and likewise toward honour and dishonour;
he should sustain himself always on alms, and never eat at one house only.

Vyāsa said:
A drinker of liquor should himself drink heated liquor burning like fire;
when his body is consumed by it, that best of the twice-born is freed.

Having killed a brāhmaṇa woman, a vipra should observe the vow for eight years;
for a kṣatriya woman, six years; for a vaiśya woman, three years;
and having slain a śūdra woman, the best of the twice-born is purified in one year.

Having killed a Vaiśya woman inadvertently, one should give something to a twice-born.
And in the case of killing outcasts, one should perform the cāndrāyaṇa observance;
or purification comes by the parāka—so said the unborn lord.

Having slain a dog, he should drink milk for three nights, fully self-controlled,
or for a cat or a mongoose walk a yojana along the road;
and for the slaying of a horse, a twice-born should perform the twelve-night kṛcchra penance.

For the slaying of elephants, the taptakṛcchra penance is prescribed as the purification;
and having killed a cow inadvertently, the cāndrāyaṇa or parāka penance;
but for the intentional slaying of a cow, no expiation is found.

Thus ends the thirty-second chapter in the latter section of the compilation of six thousand verses in the glorious Kūrma Purāṇa.

Having eaten human flesh, he should perform the cāndrāyaṇa;
and having eaten a crow, a dog, an elephant,
a boar, or a domestic fowl, he is purified by the taptakṛcchra.

And for [eating] the meat of carnivorous animals, or the feces or urine
of cows, jackals, and monkeys, he should perform the same vow;
and having fasted for twelve days, he should offer ghee with the kūṣmāṇḍa mantras.

Having eaten a mongoose, an owl, or a cat, he should perform the sāntapana;
having eaten carnivorous beasts, camels, or donkeys, he is purified by the taptakṛcchra;
and his initiatory rite [must be performed] like a vow, according to the earlier rule.

Then, seeing Rudra, Maheśa, the sole lord of all, coming not far away
with the Goddess of the gods, his fear departed;
[meanwhile] Time approached to drag off that royal seer.

He is the eternal [lord] of the gods, the sages, and the ancestors;
at the end of a thousand cosmic ages, at the dissolution of all embodied beings,
this blessed Maheśvara destroys, having become Time.

Some resemble mountains, some are like herds of elephants;
others resemble heaps of burning coals, and some carry shoals of fish;
multiform, terrible in appearance, making frightful roaring sounds.

Then all the rain-bearing clouds fill the vault of the sky;
then those roaring, terrible clouds, born from the sun,
enfolded sevenfold within themselves, extinguish that fire.

Thus the earth with its mountains and continents is gradually covered by waters;
the water drunk by the sun's rays stays in the clouds,
falls again to the earth, and by it the oceans are filled.

